#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "854a12f4-ba8e-5cfe-b044-939b33dc3086" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a System of ODEs Using Euler's Method With Multiple Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831c373c-69a5-561a-aa69-c42ac7f2354e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "831c373c-69a5-561a-aa69-c42ac7f2354e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357409} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcf18817-cc9b-570e-9112-c78380a3c24a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f46405-82cf-5473-a0f3-c2ebd3e20114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f46405-82cf-5473-a0f3-c2ebd3e20114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357411} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b7e7b2b-eee3-524e-bd51-7e9fbf0ca779" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a17e5928-0744-5389-ae84-987ca3b170c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333280" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2}-z,\\, & y(-2)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2-y+2z,\\, & z(-2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-2$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-2)=0$ and $z(-2)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-2+{0}^{2}-1=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-0+2\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-2+1=-1, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-3)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ |  |  | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-1+(-3)^{2}-5=3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-(-3)+2\\cdot 5=15, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=15\\cdot 1=15, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+3=0, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=5+15=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ | $15$ | $15$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(0)≈0$\nand\n$z(0)≈20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "854a12f4-ba8e-5cfe-b044-939b33dc3086" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a System of ODEs Using Euler's Method With Multiple Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523eb04f-e008-5aba-a683-b19269119ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357414} {:answer/id #uuid "bc358498-e85b-5902-9fa6-440aa6b4bd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc358498-e85b-5902-9fa6-440aa6b4bd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357415} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab05ee9-a1c3-56f0-9405-ed79f9919aee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04bd3d05-0c8d-5744-8340-7f6034b7afec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04bd3d05-0c8d-5744-8340-7f6034b7afec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357417} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a83ff5a-d5c3-5f16-b715-cf1177a99fb0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04180d76-0fa4-56d9-8a15-22706f13b2f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335115" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=xy+z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+2,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=0\\cdot 1+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=0-1+2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=\\frac{1}{2}-1+2=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8b20277-7fb3-5702-a295-1b1722d1cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a System of Three ODEs Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f97ed2-9349-5573-a72a-3a1586a120f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f97ed2-9349-5573-a72a-3a1586a120f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357420} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ab441e-48a8-597d-a2d4-060f1449a958" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe353281-7ea0-562d-9a64-c165c30e02fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe353281-7ea0-562d-9a64-c165c30e02fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d8350c-b98a-50a9-99c0-14269b063a5d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475323ba-b613-5ed5-a614-62bbf3a7723d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "475323ba-b613-5ed5-a614-62bbf3a7723d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357424} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1bd35b4-2199-50cf-b20b-72a895f42232" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dc67434-4d58-5098-bcfc-8265ace6ddb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335286" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2y-z+x,\\, & y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′}=z+w,\\, & z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=\\frac{xz}{2y},\\, & w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-z+x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =z+w \\\\  & =1+(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\frac{xz}{2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{16}=\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{3}{4})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=-2+\\frac{3}{16}=-\\frac{29}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{16}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{29}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{11}{16},\\,z(1)≈\\frac{1}{4},\\,w(1)≈-\\frac{29}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8b20277-7fb3-5702-a295-1b1722d1cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a System of Three ODEs Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5349169-5ff3-5cca-b80b-f0c771567b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357427} {:answer/id #uuid "003427d2-cc27-5a96-ad56-526301bf033f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5349169-5ff3-5cca-b80b-f0c771567b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357427} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee33571e-0dcd-5324-a225-249c8eab567d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e58b683b-10cb-5e17-bf06-7c8eb30b87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58b683b-10cb-5e17-bf06-7c8eb30b87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357430} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f99b100-2705-5927-bc47-f6285edd2ed4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8281e7c1-4653-5764-b26d-190189698615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8281e7c1-4653-5764-b26d-190189698615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357432} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c1a2458-65a2-5b3b-aee4-dfebe1b15fc7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2515474c-801b-502c-b2db-5ca0ed29001b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2515474c-801b-502c-b2db-5ca0ed29001b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357434} :answer-field/id #uuid "8828b337-099c-56ef-918f-d079f3010e76" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88911966-039f-58d9-957c-247714b4359c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88911966-039f-58d9-957c-247714b4359c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357436} :answer-field/id #uuid "883a41e5-748a-5a9c-9d0e-befacaad1402" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9c0cd28-0ff0-5504-9532-3903e225428a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c0cd28-0ff0-5504-9532-3903e225428a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357438} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb47b24f-ce3a-5418-9c1a-958fbb096ef4" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9eef576-a2b0-509c-997f-ce101341d373" :question/math-academy-id "q-335318" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=1+x-2y+3z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2y-w,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=-x+yz,\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1+x-2y+3z \\\\  & =1+0-2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2y-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-x+yz \\\\  & =-0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈1,$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈\\frac{3}{4},$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8b20277-7fb3-5702-a295-1b1722d1cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a System of Three ODEs Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e06c916d-66a8-5b43-a285-59e51ee47451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e06c916d-66a8-5b43-a285-59e51ee47451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357441} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66f848e-ad75-521f-a66a-fc9090722f6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdd48dc8-bca0-5f6d-9d7a-9c72a9ac545d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdd48dc8-bca0-5f6d-9d7a-9c72a9ac545d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357443} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddefb88e-070c-56b6-9b23-ec78204b390f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d9439a-0ccc-5293-9ea5-4a19ec6165f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d9439a-0ccc-5293-9ea5-4a19ec6165f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357445} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e2158f-c522-56fb-825e-e1cf081a3a15" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827bed91-f056-5d9f-aad0-194c0bd83d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "827bed91-f056-5d9f-aad0-194c0bd83d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357447} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb6e24ef-31ae-5be9-aecd-44db3da3de40" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24551815-2ea0-5978-a134-a63251217346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24551815-2ea0-5978-a134-a63251217346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357449} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbf7797-d62e-5775-9d51-36258f076839" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d21fa71c-aed1-5d5d-af11-e8124c790970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21fa71c-aed1-5d5d-af11-e8124c790970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357451} :answer-field/id #uuid "4372a66c-b35c-5500-ba60-8163ae81b373" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b85653e5-b8f8-5d29-a7bc-d47fb154cbb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335319" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2-x+z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=3y-2z+w,\\, & z(0)=-1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=1-y,\\, & w(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=-1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2-x+z \\\\  & =2-0+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3y-2z+w \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)+0 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =1-y \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+1=0 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{6}{5}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈\\frac{6}{5},$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈0,$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈0.$"}]}
